Low-level nested product/union optimization audit:
- Probed the current source normalizer and recursive operation-graph optimizer with the direct equivalent of `s(foo).iter(x, x_, s(bar).iter(y, y_, Singleton(cux.x) \/ Singleton(cux.y)))`.
- Source normalization made no changes (`SOURCE_STEPS` was empty). The optimized graph converged in two rounds but retained both nested `Iteration` subgraphs, both `Singleton` nodes, their inner `Union`, and both path `Concat` nodes. It only shared one duplicate `cux` constant.
- The source rules contain useful pieces (`iteration-union-invariant`, `concat-singleton-iteration`, `wrap-iteration`, and iteration identity), but `iteration-union-invariant` requires a provably headed source. `s(foo)` and `s(bar)` are dynamic `Unwrap(Mention(...))` expressions and do not satisfy that proof.
- With a synthetic headed sentinel added to both sources, normalization did split the nested product into the union of two independently wrapped head iterations. It did not factor the common `cux` wrap into one destination or collapse the two loops into `cux x (Head(foo) \/ Head(bar))`.
- The operation graph has no accumulator-update/destination-specialization IR corresponding to the proposed `res +=`, `res(prefix) +=`, drain-chain, single-use, or no-loop stages. `push_out` hoists loop-invariant ordinary nodes; it does not lift an accumulating iteration out of its parent. `optimize_sharing` performs local node CSE and explicitly does not share subgraphs.
- The proposed unconditional final expression is not denotationally equivalent for arbitrary sets. Counterexample: `s = {foo, bar.z}`. The outer `foo` iteration has no headed item, so the original nested product is empty, while `cux x (Head(foo) \/ Head(bar)) = {cux.z}`. The collapse is sound only when both iterated sources are proved to have at least one headed path, or when equivalent emptiness guards are retained.
- Four concrete fixtures confirmed equality when both sides were empty or both sources were headed, and exposed the stated counterexample when only `bar` was headed.

